= Climate change — Technical =

== Statistical and physical scope ==
Climate change is a persistent shift in climatic statistics over a defined region and period. Global mean surface temperature is one indicator; changes in precipitation, extremes, ocean heat content and ice mass involve additional measures and baselines.

== Drivers and attribution ==
Radiative forcing from greenhouse gases changes the balance between absorbed solar energy and outgoing terrestrial radiation. Aerosols, land cover, volcanic activity, solar forcing and internal variability also influence climate. Detection and attribution compare observed fingerprints with modeled responses and multiple independent data sources. The strength of a specific attribution claim must be reported using its original confidence and time period.

== Dated evidence ==
The IPCC's 2023 AR6 Synthesis Report estimates 2011–2020 global surface temperature at about 1.1 °C above 1850–1900. That figure is a period-specific assessment, not a measurement for November 2026.

== Interpretation limits ==
A projection requires an emissions scenario, climate-response assumptions, model ensemble and timeframe. Uncertainty in regional precipitation or a particular event does not overturn the broader physical attribution of historical warming.

